Source: tmd
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Bryan C. Everly <bryan@theeverlys.com>
# Deliberately short, and it is meant to stay that way. tmd links against
# nothing but libc: there is nothing to vendor, nothing to fetch, and a
# Launchpad builder -- which has no network -- can build it from the source
# package alone. Every entry added here is a reason a build could fail on
# somebody else's machine.
#
# Note that this stays true even though tmd reads compressed archives: it runs
# the system's decompressor as a separate process, the way GNU tar runs gzip,
# rather than linking a compression library. Those are RUNTIME dependencies,
# declared below, and none of them is needed to build.
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/bceverly/tmd
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/bceverly/tmd.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/bceverly/tmd
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: tmd
# Not "all": this is a compiled C program, so the package is tied to an
# architecture. "any" rather than a list, because nothing here is
# architecture-specific -- the build works wherever a C11 compiler does.
Architecture: any
# tmd decompresses by running the system's own tool, so the tools it runs are
# runtime dependencies rather than libraries it links.
#
# xz-utils is a Depends: it is priority:standard rather than essential, so a
# minimal system can genuinely be without it, and .tar.xz is too common for
# `apt install tmd` to leave that to chance.
#
# gzip is deliberately NOT listed. It is Essential:yes, which means it is
# present on every Debian system by definition -- so depending on it is both
# redundant and a Policy violation, and lintian says so:
# "depends-on-essential-package-without-using-version".
#
# bzip2 and zstd are Recommends: common enough that most people want them,
# rare enough that nobody should be forced to take them. The rest are Suggests.
# Without any of them tmd still works perfectly on uncompressed archives and
# says plainly which tool is missing when it meets one it cannot open.
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
 xz-utils,
Recommends:
 bzip2,
 zstd,
Suggests:
 lz4,
 lzip,
Description: dump the metadata out of a tar archive
 tmd reads a tar archive and reports what is inside it without extracting
 anything. By default it prints one ls(1)-style line per member: the permission
 bits, the owner and group, the size, the modification time the archive
 recorded, and the path.
 .
 BSD archives (ustar and pax, as bsdtar and libarchive write them), GNU tar
 archives including long names and sparse files, and pre-POSIX v7 archives are
 all understood. The format is worked out per member rather than per archive,
 because a real archive mixes them.
 .
 Beyond the listing it can report every header field in long, JSON or CSV form;
 verify header checksums and the end-of-archive marker; and describe the
 archive itself -- which generation of the format it belongs to, which
 extensions it relies on, what wrote it, and what it would take to read it
 somewhere else.
 .
 Compressed archives are read directly: a .tar.gz, .tar.xz, .tar.bz2, .tar.zst
 and several others are recognized by content and unpacked on the way in, by
 running the system's own decompressor as a separate process rather than by
 linking a compression library.
 .
 The archive is only ever opened for reading. Only headers are read, and member
 data is seeked over, so the cost of reading an archive is proportional to how
 many members it has rather than to how large it is.
